A PRODUCT TYPE OF THE JAVASCRIPT PROFILE THAT DECLARES A FINALIZER, so rule N25's rejecting
direction is decided by an input rather than by a comment. It is written the way the regression
would arrive: a `FinalizationRegistry` that wants its cleanup callbacks to "just run" reaches for
the CLR's own hook, which runs on the collector's thread, at a moment no guest chose, outside every
budget.

It declares a finalizer both ways the language allows - the destructor syntax and an override of
`Finalize` - and it also NAMES `~JsRegistry()` and `Finalize()` in a comment and in a string
literal, which the rule must not report: it reads declarations, so prose that explains the
prohibition is not a violation of it.

```csharp
namespace Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript;

internal sealed class JsRegistryWithAFinalizer
{
    // Not ~JsRegistryWithAFinalizer() and not Finalize(): that is what the rule is about.
    private const string Explanation = "~JsRegistry() and Finalize() are forbidden";

    ~JsRegistryWithAFinalizer()
    {
    }
}

internal class JsRegistryBase
{
}

internal sealed class JsRegistryWithAnOverride : JsRegistryBase
{
#pragma warning disable CS0465
    protected void Finalize()
#pragma warning restore CS0465
    {
    }
}
```

This file is a witness and is never compiled. It lives here rather than in a product project for
the reason every rejecting witness does: a file that would make the rule fail cannot live where the
rule looks.
